#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176736} :activity/id #uuid "d78cd3be-1d2a-523a-8697-c68f98a589f3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049576 :topic/id #uuid "01545133-7608-516c-bc2b-72cca5097adb" :topic/math-academy-id 3629} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176997 :tutorial/content "Not every square matrix is diagonalizable. However, if a square matrix is not diagonalizable, it is \"almost\" diagonalizable. \n\nMore precisely, every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan matrix, and $P$ is an invertible matrix. We say that $J$ is a **Jordan canonical form** (or **Jordan normal form**) of $A.$\n\nNot all square matrices have corresponding diagonal matrices. However, *all* square matrices have Jordan canonical forms. A Jordan canonical form is either diagonal or \"almost\" diagonal. \n\nOur goal in this lesson is to find a Jordan canonical form of any $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nIf a matrix is diagonalizable, then finding its Jordan canonical form is equivalent to finding its diagonal form. \n\nFor diagonalizable $2\\times 2$ matrices, we have two separate cases:\n\n- $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues.\n\n- $A$ is a **scalar matrix**. A diagonal matrix is scalar if every value on the leading diagonal is the same.\n\nLet's discuss these two cases first. Then, we will discuss the final case, which is when $A$ is not diagonalizable." :tutorial/id #uuid "08eeaf45-d9aa-50e2-a1d4-64ee464c4729" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5439 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a91a4bcf-6390-569f-b6db-61d6d0af3359" :step/math-academy-id 33887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176737}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175988 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175839 :question/id #uuid "e1c44d04-1798-5619-813d-a75e8f05140d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9087"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f20482-73ea-5f1c-bb2b-1d7472fbfd4e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050001}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386411}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Two Distinct Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "1babc62c-bf05-5b82-9cc3-3c1c5db69824" :step/math-academy-id 33888 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176775}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176773 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177015 :tutorial/content "When a $2\\times 2$ matrix with repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ is not diagonalizable, its Jordan canonical form is given by\n\n$$\nJ = J_2(\\lambda).\n$$\n\nAs an example, let's find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe first find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=3.$ \n\nSince $A$ has a repeated eigenvalue and is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable. \n\nTherefore, in this case, the Jordan canonical form of $A$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that $J$ is \"almost\" diagonal." :tutorial/id #uuid "70056323-b6a3-52d6-8ebc-22f6d493088b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5584 :tutorial/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a Non-Diagonalizable 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "ec6918dd-4d6e-5fd7-b37e-24cbfecd322f" :step/math-academy-id 34482 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176774}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176008 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175861 :question/id #uuid "d60be4d7-335c-564a-bc75-52a2f76d660f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9225"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b629bead-e64a-5e73-ae42-80d4ebb3762b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049113}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386633}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Only One Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "06ead4b8-8dc4-5c4b-9215-eb8ae6101d9f" :step/math-academy-id 34489 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176778}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176009 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175862 :question/id #uuid "16a3033b-9110-5e1f-914d-8351bdc5f930" :question/math-academy-id "e-9226"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "511398e8-780e-57b2-8f1b-092e9f6cca45" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386620}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Scalar Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "f282e279-40b8-542c-86e5-6dbd39bf2e2c" :step/math-academy-id 34490 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176773}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176778 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177017 :tutorial/content "Given a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ we have $3$ possible cases:\n\n- If $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 \\neq \\lambda_2,$ then $A$ is diagonalizable, and $J$ is a diagonal matrix. In this case, $J$ is a composition of two distinct $1 \\times 1$ Jordan blocks:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- If $A$ has only one eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$ and there exist two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, then $A$ must be a scalar matrix. This situation corresponds to the case where $A$ is already diagonalized, and $J$ is a composition of two identical $1 \\times 1$ Jordan blocks:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- If $A$ has only one eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector exists that corresponds to this eigenvalue, then $A$ is not diagonalizable. In this case, $J$ is a single $2 \\times 2$ Jordan block:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, in two out of three cases, our matrix is diagonalizable. But in the third case, the closest we can get to a diagonal matrix is a Jordan block with a $1$ on the superdiagonal." :tutorial/id #uuid "56909b69-a1ff-5904-931a-bb0ed2f8da72" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5597 :tutorial/title "Overview of All Possible Jordan Canonical Forms of 2x2 Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "72da1946-1276-5e5b-935a-3b2250f7ee5e" :step/math-academy-id 34509}] :activity/title "Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176147}]}
